<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Twelver Shi'ism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Twelver Shiʿism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Twelver <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiism">Shi'ism</a> is the largest branch of Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, recognising a line of twelve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a>, the last of whom is believed to be in occultation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhan">Ilkhan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oljeitu">Öljeitü</a>'s conversion to Twelver Shi'ism briefly promoted it within state structures. This laid cultural foundations for the later Safavid establishment of Twelver Shi'ism as the state religion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism.jpg" alt="Twelver Shi'ism" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Safa.daneshvar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:001123-AbolfazlShrine-BetweenTheHolyShrines-Karbala-IMG_7729-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nader attempted one significant institutional innovation: at the Mughan assembly in 1736 he proposed that <b>Twelver Shi'ism</b> be recognised as a fifth school of Sunni jurisprudence, the Ja'fari madhhab, which would have positioned Iran as a bridge between Sunni and Shi'a Islam and eased the integration of his…</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Under their rule <b>Twelver Shia</b> scholarship flourished in Baghdad, with al-Shaykh al-Mufid, al-Sharif al-Murtada and al-Sharif al-Radi, compiler of the Nahj al-Balagha.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ilkhanate also influenced the religious landscape of Persia: Öljeitü's conversion to <b>Twelver Shiʿism</b> briefly promoted the sect within state structures, an early precedent for the Safavid establishment of Shiʿism as Persia's official religion in 1501.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Twelver Shi’a Islam</b> was the state religion, and the Shi’i clergy, the ulama, gained great independence and influence, since leading jurists, or mujtahids, were regarded as authorities whom believers should follow.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…and the act that most decisively shaped subsequent Iranian and Middle Eastern history, was his immediate proclamation of <b>Twelver Shia Islam</b> as the state religion of Iran, a radical imposition in a region that was predominantly Sunni and had little recent tradition of Shia rule at the state…</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty was <b>Twelver Shi'i</b> ruling a largely Sunni population and taxed heavily to fund war.</blockquote><a href="/state/hamdanid-emirate-of-aleppo">Hamdanid Emirate of Aleppo</a></li></ul></section>
